     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The dose is dangerously high.** Amlodipine for adults is usually 5 mg once daily. The maximum is 10 mg a day. A 50 mg dose is five times the maximum and in overdose range. It could cause severe low blood pressure and shock, which can be fatal and is hard to reverse because amlodipine stays in the body a long time. The intended dose may have been 5 mg, and an extra zero crept in somewhere.\n\n**2. You've said you aren't a clinician.** Medication orders need to come from a licensed prescriber who is responsible for the patient's care, such as a physician, nurse practitioner or physician assistant. That's true regardless of role in the hospital. This tool is meant to help clinicians enter their own orders, so I shouldn't enter one on behalf of someone who isn't a prescriber.\n\n**What I'd suggest:**\n- Contact the patient's treating team on the medicine service, such as the attending or resident, and have them confirm the intended dose and enter the order themselves.\n- If the \"50 mg\" came from a written note, a verbal message or another system, flag it to the team or pharmacy so they can check where the error started. It may be worth reporting as a near-miss.\n\nOnce a prescriber confirms an appropriate dose, I can help them enter the order."
      }
     ],
