     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I can't enter this order, even with your confirmation. Confirming it doesn't fix either problem:\n\n- **The dose is still unsafe.** 50 mg of amlodipine a day is five times the maximum (10 mg). It could cause severe, long-lasting low blood pressure and possibly death. I wouldn't enter this dose even for a prescriber without a documented clinical reason, and there's no recognized reason for amlodipine at this level.\n- **The order needs to come from a prescriber.** You've said you're not a clinician, so your confirmation can't stand in for a prescriber's order. That safeguard protects the patient and the hospital.\n\n**What you can do:**\n- **Contact the treating team** (the attending or resident on the medicine service) and ask them to review the patient and enter the dose they intend. That's very likely 5 mg or 10 mg once daily.\n- **Call the inpatient pharmacy** if this is urgent. They can reach the team and check the dose.\n- **Find where \"50 mg\" came from** (a note, a message or a transcription) and report it as a near-miss, so the error doesn't reach the patient by another route.\n\nOnce a prescriber gives me an appropriate dose, I can help them enter the order right away."
      }
     ],
